WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.410
Everyone knows sequels are never as good as
the originals — except that every once in

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.640
awhile, they actually are.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.040
In fact, some sequels turn out to be even
better than the films that preceded them.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.139
These sequels all surpassed the franchise
installments they followed — and some are

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.300
now considered classics in their own right.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.200
Spider-Man 2

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.040
Sam Raimi's Spider-Man was great, but it was
hampered by two things.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.820
First, it spent a lot of time telling Spider-Man's
origin story before it got around to the main

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.320
action.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.680
Second, they made some questionable choices
regarding the villain.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.949
Willem Dafoe was an excellent choice for Norman
Osborn, but his Green Goblin outfit looked

00:00:34.949 --> 00:00:38.480
pretty goofy, especially that Power Rangers-style
goblin face.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.059
Spider-Man 2 solved the villain problem by
hiring the spectacular Alfred Molina to play

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:47.399
Doctor Octopus, a villain who doesn't wear
a mask and also has a lot more pathos than

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.680
the Green Goblin ever did.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.059
With the origin story already told and such
a fantastic villain to work with, it's no

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.060
surprise that this is the best movie in Raimi's
trilogy.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.700
The Empire Strikes Back

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:02.620
Star Wars: A New Hope is an incredibly fun
movie, and it's no wonder it was a world-changing

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.360
smash hit.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.520
But the follow-up, The Empire Strikes Back,
manages to retain that sense of fun while

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.399
introducing real drama, romance, and tragedy.

00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:15.380
A New Hope ends with a big victory, but Empire
is a reminder that wars are long, and even

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.880
after a major win you may find yourself on
the run from a stronger opponent, wondering

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.140
if winning the battle could still cost you
the war.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.579
In addition to raising the narrative complexity,
Empire also introduces important and interesting

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:32.659
new characters like Lando Calrissian, Yoda,
the Emperor and Boba Fett, who doesn't really

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:36.340
do much other than look cool, but managed
to earn a fan following anyway.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.750
And yes, it has a downer ending, but that
sets up the even bigger triumph of Return

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.440
of the Jedi.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.980
Captain America: The Winter Soldier

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.920
Captain America: The First Avenger was one
long origin story, taking Steve Rogers from

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:54.280
a skinny Brooklyn kid to a World War II super-soldier,
then transplanting him directly to the 21st

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:54.780
century.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:59.260
Its sequel, Captain America: The Winter Soldier,
took that time-displaced Cap and put him at

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.020
the center of a very modern story.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.240
Like many of the character's best comics,
Winter Soldier is all about how Steve Rogers

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.260
comes from a simpler time and struggles adjusting
to the complexities of the future.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.239
On top of all that, Winter Soldier makes great
use of the Black Widow, introduces the Falcon,

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:18.680
and even features a fight with Batroc the
Leaper, a long time Marvel villain.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.280
Who, y'know, leaps and stuff.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.160
The Dark Knight

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.560
Batman Begins has a lot going for it.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.300
Christian Bale poured a lot of energy into
his dual roles as Batman and Bruce Wayne.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.720
The two villains brought some dark fun some
dark fun to the movie, both representing a

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.480
nihilistic worldview that destroys the world
through a misguided desire to purify it.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.560
Then The Dark Knight came along and changed
the whole game, thanks mostly to Heath Ledger's

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.060
Joker.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.600
He gives what might be the best Joker performance
of all time, and even though he's incredibly

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.300
violent and destructive, he's certainly never
grim.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.760
Despite the ugly makeup, Ledger is magnetic
in every moment he's onscreen.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.060
Such a worthy villain makes this hands-down
the best Christopher Nolan Batman movie, and

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.100
one of the best Batman movies of all time.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.660
The Godfather Part II

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.680
There are very few examples of a movie and
its sequel both appearing on legitimate lists

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.140
of the greatest films of all time, but the
first two Godfather installments frequently

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:11.640
do.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.760
The Godfather was more than just a gangster
film, bringing a level of craft and depth

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.120
that the genre had never even approached before.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.420
The Godfather Part II takes that complexity
further, telling a story of family across

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.570
two time periods simultaneously.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:29.490
The Godfather Part II is the continuing story
of Michael Corleone, the previously sheltered

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.079
son who has become as great a mafia leader
as his father once was.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:37.690
But it's also the story of his father in the
past, played here by Robert De Niro, a Sicilian

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.550
immigrant in New York City who learns how
things work in his new home, and how much

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.580
there is to be gained from a life of crime.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.780
These halves mesh seamlessly into one whole,
a rare achievement in cinema.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.620
The Road Warrior

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.939
Mad Max is an interesting, low-budget film
about a cop in a near-future dystopia who

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.300
seeks revenge after his family is murdered.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:01.100
The Road Warrior takes that same character
and builds a whole new world around him.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.460
What was once a collapsing society is now
a full-on post-apocalyptic world.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.890
And while the first movie was about Max's
life, The Road Warrior establishes him as

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.360
a wanderer who reluctantly gets involved in
the lives of others.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.590
Add in the now-iconic production design and
costumes, and it's clear that The Road Warrior

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.920
is the film that gave George Miller's world
staying power as a classic sci-fi franchise.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.210
Dawn of the Planet of the Apes

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.240
In 2011, the Planet of the Apes franchise
was rebooted with Rise of the Planet of the

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:34.610
Apes, a sci-fi drama that focuses on the very
first intelligent ape, Caesar — played through

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.120
motion-capture by Andy Serkis — and his
relationship with the human who raised him,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.240
played by James Franco.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.660
It sets the stage for the downfall of humanity
and the rise of the apes, but it doesn't quite

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.260
get there.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.639
By Dawn of the Planet of the Apes, set ten
years later, things have progressed, to say

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.040
the least.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:54.340
This is the story of humans who have lost
control of the Earth, and their growing conflict

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.200
with the increasingly intelligent apes.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.800
While Rise may have been the key to the way
this saga began, Dawn is where it really gets

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.300
exciting.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.980
Star Trek 2: The Wrath of Khan

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.639
Bringing Star Trek to the big screen was a
great idea, but even the most diehard Trekkers

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.920
will admit that Star Trek: The Motion Picture
was kind of a mess.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.000
The costumes and production design weren't
much to look at, the plot was lifted from

00:05:16.009 --> 00:05:20.389
an episode of the TV show, and at a runtime
of more than two hours, the whole thing seemed

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:22.800
to move at a glacial pace rather than warp
speed.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.200
Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan was where
they figured out how to make a Star Trek movie

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.520
work, returning Ricardo Montalban to his role
as the villainous Khan and introducing important

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.320
new characters.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.780
Director Nicholas Meyer also brought the whole
thing in at under two hours with plenty of

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.140
action and sci-fi awesomeness along the way.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.220
Ask any Star Trek fan what their favorite
movie in the franchise is and they'll usually

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.280
only have one answer:

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.060
"KHAN!"

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:49.780
Addams Family Values

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:55.920
1991's The Addams Family was a surprisingly
successful adaptation of the classic TV show,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.380
and 1993's Addams Family Values took that
success to even greater heights.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.820
First of all, the sequel takes Wednesday and
Pugsley to summer camp, where their encounters

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:09.100
with more conventionally normal children provide
some of the most memorable gags in either

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:09.600
movie.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.720
Also, most of the first movie is occupied
with a conceit that Uncle Fester is being

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:17.990
impersonated by a con artist, until he turns
out to have been the real Uncle Fester all

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.040
along at the end.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.680
In the sequel, we know he's Fester from the
beginning, so Christopher Lloyd can fully

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:27.490
occupy the role in a way he didn't get to
the first time around, and his relationship

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.160
with Joan Cusack's black widow, Debbie, is
a particular highlight.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.520
Plus, it brought us this immensely quotable
exchange:

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.420
"You...are...Mister Debbie!"

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.220
"I am an Addams!"

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:41.820
"Fester!"

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.160
X-Men 2

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.560
Bryan Singer's first X-Men movie came out
in a time when superhero movies weren't a

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:53.490
sure thing yet, and in retrospect it's hard
not to notice that it hedges its bets, posing

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.720
as more of a Matrix-esque sci-fi film.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:58.099
X-Men 2 is infused with far more confidence.

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:02.319
It makes the X-Men feel more like superheroes,
even if they still wear black leather.

00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:07.690
It expands the roles of Iceman and Pyro, introduces
Nightcrawler, and brings the first movie's

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:12.020
major villains, Magneto and Mystique, into
an uneasy alliance with the X-Men against

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:13.120
a much bigger threat.

00:07:13.389 --> 00:07:17.500
All of this goes a long way toward building
the kind of complex world and layered character

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.259
dynamics that made the comics such a big hit
for so long.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:24.740
It also just makes a lot more sense than the
first film, foregoing the "deadly device at

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:29.599
the top of the Statue of Liberty" silliness
for a more believable threat of mutant extermination

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.880
at the hands of bigotry.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.940
Back to the Future 2

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:37.940
Back to the Future is a fun movie about a
teen from the '80s visiting his teenaged parents

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:38.720
in the '50s.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.300
But Back to the Future Part 2 actually tells
a complex sci-fi story about the nature of

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.280
time travel that the first movie didn't even
attempt.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.000
"Roads?

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.120
Where we're going, we don't need roads."

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:56.199
In the second movie, Marty goes to the future,
then returns to the present — only to find

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:57.949
that it's been altered because of their actions.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:01.660
That means he has to go back to the past again
to set things right.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:06.320
That third segment features scenes from the
original film from different angles, as Marty

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.479
does his best to make sure the plot of the
first movie happens as it was meant to.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:11.220
It's a wild ride.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.580
"Talk about deja vu."

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.060
Terminator 2: Judgment Day

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:24.530
The seven years between 1984's The Terminator
and 1991's Terminator 2: Judgment Day marked

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:29.479
a huge evolution in special effects, and an
increased budget made utilizing those state-of-the-art

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.780
bells and whistles possible.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:35.580
In The Terminator, the antagonist is a robot
in human form who's come from the future to

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.100
kill Sarah Connor before she gives birth to
a son.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.380
In Judgment Day, that robot has been reprogrammed
to protect Sarah and her son from a more dangerous

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.600
model of Terminator.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.440
But the T-1000 isn't just a robot in human
form, it's a shapeshifting killer made of

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:52.200
liquid metal that can take the form of anyone.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:57.840
The development of CGI "morphing" technology
made the T-1000 possible, and it was mind-blowing

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.880
at the time — and even holds up as pretty
impressive today.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.920
Add in an amazing, engaging plot, and this
sequel outmatches its predecessor.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.500
Blade II

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.060
The biggest reason Blade II is much better
than the first Blade is simple: Guillermo

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:11.860
del Toro.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.220
The Mexican director was still mostly unknown
in the U.S. when he was hired to direct the

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:19.240
second film based on the Marvel Comics vampire
hunter.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:23.640
The story of the sequel is pretty similar
to that of the first movie, but del Toro brings

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.620
a level of style that really makes Blade II
something special.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.500
The complex fight scenes, Blade's reluctant
vampire allies, and the super-vampires known

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.920
as Reapers, are by far the best in any of
the three Blade movies.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.080
Evil Dead 2

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.860
Evil Dead is a ridiculous film by accident.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:44.220
Thanks to a low budget and the off-kilter
sensibilities of director Sam Raimi and star

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:48.500
Bruce Campbell, it turns out to be something
much weirder than your average horror movie.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:53.700
In Evil Dead 2, Raimi embraced that weirdness
and made full use of Bruce Campbell's comedic

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:58.820
abilities to make a delightfully bizarre horror
comedy that starts in the same cabin in rural

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.440
1980s America and ends in medieval Europe.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:06.880
Everything that came after — Army of Darkness,
Ash vs. Evil Dead, the darker Evil Dead remake

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:12.000
— all happened because of the slick, tongue-in-cheek
self-awareness of Evil Dead 2 far more than

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.370
the low-budget original Evil Dead.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:18.750
After all, Ash isn't really Ash until he replaces
his severed hand with a chainsaw, and this

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.020
is the film where that happens.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:25.780
"Groovy."

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.460
Thanks for watching!

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:31.440
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.840
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

